before we find louisiana republican  senator medical dr bill cassidy  who's part of the bipartisan working  group that's rolled out the two main  proposals senator i appreciate you being  with us and taking a  brief respite from working the phones uh  where are we exactly  so the majority leader mitch mcconnell  spoke today  he sounded optimistic they're narrowing  down the numbers of  issues that are really sticking points  down to one or two  i do anticipate based on what i've been  told that we will have a deal by this  evening  and probably vote on it sometime  tomorrow we've  we've heard for so long from a number of  folks up on capitol hill i'll take the  republican side for  for just a minute but from mitch  mcconnell how important  indemnifying and dealing with  coronavirus  lawsuits are for businesses and  protecting lawsuits from liability  protecting businesses from liability and  now that appears as though it may or may  not be in the bill people seem to be  willing to  give up so much right now just to get a  bill did  does that sort of call into question how  necessary those things were  so we had a working group of four  five democrats four or five republicans  the republicans advocating for that sort  of liability relief  for this last year when the guidelines  were very unclear  but democrats really didn't want to go  there and whatever we offered still went  too far for them  in terms of liability relief now  democrats wanted state and local aid  we linked those two together but  nonetheless although we had a deal on  state and local  we never got a liability at some point  you got to declare a victory and come  home and we were  just we got what we could we got three  for the employers for the employees for  the unemployed  we couldn't get liability relief we have  to work on that next congress there  seems to be this riff  uh in in the senate right now over  these additional checks you've got josh  hawley uh saying he wants  twelve hundred dollar checks another  round uh siding with bernie sanders uh  hard to find two people who conceivably  would be  farther apart and then your colleague  ron johnson said this last night take a  listen  this is all borrowed money against our  children's future and we would have more  money left over to target relief to the  people who really need it right now look  at all the small businesses that didn't  get  loans didn't get relief look at the  number of people that didn't do that so  congress it's easy to shovel money out  it's just easy to just  pick a number and and say let's spend  that it's a little  more difficult to actually look at the  numbers figure out the best way to  target this  and that's what doesn't happen very  often here in congress fair critique  fair critique i'll say what senator  sanders and senator hawley both said  that they would oppose money going to  small employers to keep people  employed and oppose money going for  unemployment for those who are  unemployed  and money going for food relief and  money going for rental relief  and helping landlords too because we  didn't get checks  now the checks are okay but the folks  who are really hurting  are the folks who are about to lose  their business and laying off folks who  otherwise would be  employed i say the best stimulus check  is a paycheck  so i think there's a little bit of a  different philosophy there  if we can you know if checks are part of  the deal they'll be part of the deal  i think ron johnson's point was let's  take care of the folks who are really  hurting  the unemployed the folks about to lose  their business and therefore folks about  to lose their jobs  when you've got a republican and josh  hawley uh advocating for this and  president trump has talked about checks  as well  uh just just sending out money to  everybody  is have republicans sort of lost the  north star of fiscal conservatism  so those two do not define all all of  conservatism uh nor all of the  republican party  right so we have to first and there's  always going to be diversity of opinion  now those checks are stimulatory people  are going to spend that money  and that does increase sales tax revenue  and keep stores afloat that sort of  thing  i'm not arguing there's not a benefit  there but if you're talking about the  folks as who are of greatest need  they are the folks about to lose their  job because the person who owns a small  business is about to go bankrupt  so if we have to choose where we help  first let's help the folks about to lose  their job  about to go bankrupt because i think  that neil cavuto asked  such an important question earlier of me  and i admit that  the only answer i could give is it's  2020 and anything can happen but  that caveat aside i'll put the same  question to you  which is is there any way there's not a  deal  by tomorrow by tomorrow are we going to  be going  another few days on this i think there  is a deal by tomorrow  uh what would this what would derail a  deal i'm sorry  what would you provisions that folks are  striving to get  common ground on compromise which would  be what  um there's a there's a little technical  but the under the cares act the federal  reserve was given this ability to lend  money to businesses right  we felt as republicans that was an  emergency issue  now that the emergency is resolved for  the bigger businesses we should stand  that down  democrats wish to keep it kind of a  technical point  but it's a sticking point right now well  in such an important one you can just  imagine how the markets will react on  monday either way  uh to that uh in terms of in terms of  looking at how businesses are going to  be able to survive and  get more loans from the federal  government i shouldn't say survive but  get more money from the federal  government which uh certainly changed  things  senator we appreciate it and we'll let  you get back to the phones and to work  thank you very much appreciate the time

